- How would you test and roll this out with a mixed-version producer pool?
- How would you scale the dedupe cache across multiple consumer instances?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests memory management in a KCNA sink under idle-key TTL cleanup, ensuring at-least-once processing remains correct after eviction and offsets stay coherent.

## Key Concepts

- Per-key TTL cleanup
- In-memory state with concurrency
- Offset management and idempotence
- Canary-based testing

## Code Example

```javascript
class TTLStateStore {
  constructor(ttlMs) {
    this.ttlMs = ttlMs;
    this.map = new Map();
  }
  processMessage(key, value, t) {
    this.map.set(key, { last: t, value });
  }
  commitOffsets(offsets) {
    // persist offsets
  }
  evictStaleStates(now) {
    for (const [k, v] of this.map.entries()) {
      if (now - v.last > this.ttlMs) this.map.delete(k);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would clock skew affect eviction?
- How to tune TTL for different workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design realistic, scalable A/B testing in KCNA with strict tenant isolation, deterministic sampling, and auditable results.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data partitioning and sandbox isolation
- Deterministic per-tenant sampling and evaluation
- Auditing, rollback, and compliance proofs

## Code Example
```javascript
function sampleTenant(tenantId, weight) {
  // Simple 32-bit hash derived seed
  const seed = hash32(tenantId);
  const r = (seed * 1664525 + 1013904223) >>> 0;
  return (r / 4294967296) < weight;
}
```

